{
  "_comment": "Curated content for the Positions & Legal Status page (/api/positions -> PositionsTab). HAND-CURATED ONLY — never AI-generate or bulk-edit content in this file; misstating a legal position is the same credibility risk class as misattributing a key-figure quote. Editorial stance: DESCRIPTIVE, NOT ADJUDICATIVE — every claim is an actor's STATED position anchored to a dated primary source (statute text, communique, official transcript; news only as fallback); the page never rules on which position is correct. Prose is English; every key formulation carries the original Chinese (_zh) plus romanisation — Wade-Giles/Tongyong for Taiwan-coined terms, Hanyu Pinyin for PRC-coined terms, per house rules. Script convention: simplified characters for PRC-origin text, traditional for Taiwan-origin and third-party text. Empty-string fields render as 'entry pending' placeholders; fill them in co-writing sessions. tests/test_positions_content.py validates this file's shape on every pytest run — run it after editing.",

  "_meta": {
    "version": 1,
    "last_reviewed": "2026-07-10"
  },

  "_comment_actors": "One object per actor. depth 'full' renders expandable sections (legal_foundation / current_position / sub_positions / misconceptions); depth 'brief' renders a single compact card from intro_en + current_position. legal_foundation = what is legally or constitutionally on the books (changes rarely; anchored on effective_date). current_position = the actor's current official line (changes a few times a year; each item anchored to a dated formulation via as_stated + stated_by). not_say_en / nuance_en are the 'what this is commonly assumed to say but doesn't' fields — they are the point of the page, use them. sub_positions = intra-actor splits (Taiwan's DPP/KMT/TPP); 'party' keys into frontend/src/partyColours.js PARTY_COLOURS. hooks = future live integrations, rendered by nothing in v1. sources[] items are {label, url, date, lang}.",

